//RS 
//RS /// D-ENC-DYN1=A+ (ratified 2026-06-25) + D-SERDE13=B (ratified 2026-07-11): the
//RS /// one dynamic encoding value every format's `parse` returns and every hand codec
//RS /// constructs and returns. `DataTree` is the single canonical user-facing
//RS /// spelling — renamed from the old `Data` face by D-SERDE13=B (it is a tree of
//RS /// data, distinct from any user type named `Data`; the retired `Data` spelling is
//RS /// a teaching error, E0351, not an alias, per I8). `JSON`/`TOML`/`YAML`/`CSV` are
//RS /// format-tagged aliases over the same structure, so `json.parse` is typed
//RS /// `JSON`, `toml.parse` is typed `TOML`, etc., but one walker and one accessor
//RS /// set back them. Variants: `Null`, `Bool`, `Int`, `Float`, `Text`, `Array`,
//RS /// `Object`.
//RS pub const TYPE_DATA: &str = "DataTree";
//RS /// D-SERDE16=A (ratified 2026-07-11): public target-directed subtree dispatch.
//RS /// `tree.decode<T>()` calls only `T`'s ordinary `Decode` protocol impl.
//RS pub const METHOD_DATATREE_DECODE: &str = "decode";
//RS pub const TYPE_DATA_JSON: &str = "JSON";
//RS pub const TYPE_DATA_TOML: &str = "TOML";
//RS pub const TYPE_DATA_YAML: &str = "YAML";
//RS pub const TYPE_DATA_CSV: &str = "CSV";
//RS 
//RS /// The accepted spellings of the dynamic encoding value (D-ENC-DYN1=A+ /
//RS /// D-SERDE13=B): canonical `DataTree` plus the four format-tagged aliases. The
